  "scientific_result": "Detectability fix (orbit rate 90 deg/sec, chosen by pilot sweep -- see orbit_rate_selection): real_motion's representation-change ratio E||Z'-Z||^2 / E_cross-scene||Zi-Zj||^2 = 0.3643 at N=100 (static_control, UNCHANGED motion, = 0.0000). On held-out test scenes of the real_motion condition, the linear map W_T fit between temporal window t1 and window t2 achieved R^2=0.504, exceeding the best of the three Task-6-style controls (R^2=0.356) by 0.149, but did NOT exceed the identical protocol's static-clip control (R^2=0.878; margin=-0.374). Per tasks/12_temporal_consistency.md, beating the standard baselines is not sufficient here -- a positive result requires beating the static-clip control specifically, since the static-clip control is the primary tool for ruling out that temporal windowing/adjacent-frame smoothness alone (not genuine motion) produced this apparent consistency. This is a valid negative result for the 'beyond-the-static-control' hypothesis: no measurable temporal consistency attributable to camera motion itself was found beyond what windowing alone already gives, under this motion type and windowing scheme.",
